    {"id": 1, "claim": "Radio waves are electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths much longer than visible light, making them invisible to the human eye.", "evidence": "The visible spectrum is approximately 400-700 nm, while radio waves range from 1 mm to kilometers.", "weight": 0.10, "depends_on": []},
    {"id": 2, "claim": "Radio waves occupy the low-frequency end of the electromagnetic spectrum compared to visible light, X-rays, and gamma rays.", "evidence": "Frequency and wavelength are inversely proportional; longer wavelengths correspond to lower frequencies.", "weight": 0.10, "depends_on": []},
    {"id": 3, "claim": "The radio signal is generated by an electronic transmitter in the collar, not by biological processes in the animal.", "evidence": "The problem states the collars 'emit' radio waves, implying an artificial source.", "weight": 0.10, "depends_on": []},
    {"id": 4, "claim": "Standard tracking collars use omnidirectional antennas that radiate energy in all horizontal directions.", "evidence": "Wildlife telemetry typically uses dipole or monopole antennas which have a 360-degree radiation pattern in the horizontal plane.", "weight": 0.30, "depends_on": [3]},
    {"id": 5, "claim": "The omnidirectional propagation allows a tracker to detect the signal from any bearing without needing to align with the animal or have direct line-of-sight.", "evidence": "If the signal only went in one direction, the tracker would need to be in a specific spot; spreading in all directions makes the source detectable from multiple locations, enabling triangulation.", "weight": 0.40, "depends_on": [4]},
    {"id": 6, "claim": "Attacking the weakest link: Even if one argues that 'spreading in all directions' is a property of all EM waves (including light), it is the *practical* reason for choosing radio waves over visible light for tracking, because radio waves also penetrate obstacles and travel further, but among the given options, C is the only one that describes a functional advantage of the wave's propagation relative to the tracking task.", "evidence": "Option A is factually false. Option B is factually misleading (radio is low freq) and not the primary reason (high freq is often worse for range). Option D is factually false. Option C is the only statement that is both true and directly relevant to the utility of the tracking method.", "weight": 0.10, "depends_on": [1, 2, 5]}
